    "created_at": "2026-10-20T16:10:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-20T16:10:27Z",
    "body": "I think the key distinction here is that `path_type` shouldn't just control the returned value — it should also be the object used for the checks when one is provided.\n\nRight now `convert()` runs `os.stat()` / `os.access()` on the original value and only calls `coerce_path_result()` at the end. That makes `path_type=cloudpathlib.AnyPath` ineffective for `exists`, `file_okay`, `dir_okay`, etc. because those checks are still against the local filesystem.\n\nIt seems reasonable to coerce the value first when `path_type` is set, then use the resulting object's path semantics for the checks, while keeping the current `os.*` behavior for the default local-path case.\n\nThat would also make `path_type` behave more like an actual path abstraction rather than only a return-type conversion.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
